1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,520
Sally: Good morning. Hinchingbrooke Country Park

2
00:00:03,240 --> 00:00:06,260
Sally speaking. I'm one of the rangers.

3
00:00:06,260 --> 00:00:09,500
John: Oh hello. My name's John Chapman

4
00:00:09,500 --> 00:00:12,742
and I'm a teaching assistant at a local primary school.

5
00:00:12,742 --> 00:00:18,200
I've been asked to arrange a visit to the park for two of our classes.

6
00:00:18,200 --> 00:00:21,270
Sally: OK. What would you like to know?

7
00:00:21,270 --> 00:00:24,140
John: Well, I'm new to this area

8
00:00:24,200 --> 00:00:28,560
so perhaps you could tell me something about the park first, please.

9
00:00:28,560 --> 00:00:37,460
Sally: Of course. Altogether the park covers 170 acres, that's 69 hectares.

10
00:00:37,460 --> 00:00:43,620
Sally: There are three main types of habitat: wetland, grassland and woodland.

11
00:00:43,620 --> 00:00:46,340
Sally: The woods are well established and varied

12
00:00:46,420 --> 00:00:51,040
with an oak plantation, and other areas of mixed species.

13
00:00:51,040 --> 00:00:52,070
John: Right.

14
00:00:52,070 --> 00:00:55,238
Sally: The wetland is quite varied, too.

15
00:00:55,238 --> 00:01:01,120
The original farmland was dug up around 40 years ago to extract gravel.

16
00:01:01,120 --> 00:01:02,940
Sally: Once this work was completed

17
00:01:03,540 --> 00:01:08,440
the gravel pits filled with water, forming the two large lakes.

18
00:01:08,440 --> 00:01:10,820
Sally: There are also several smaller ones

19
00:01:11,180 --> 00:01:15,300
ponds and a stream that flows through the park.

20
00:01:15,300 --> 00:01:22,640
John: OK, so I suppose with these different habitats there's quite a variety of wildlife.

21
00:01:22,640 --> 00:01:28,854
Sally: There certainly is - a lot of different species of birds and insects,

22
00:01:28,854 --> 00:01:32,200
and also animals like deer and rabbits.

23
00:01:32,200 --> 00:01:37,280
John: And I understand you organise educational visits for school parties.

24
00:01:37,280 --> 00:01:41,780
Sally: That's right. We can organise a wide range of activities

25
00:01:41,780 --> 00:01:44,870
and adapt them to suit all ages.

26
00:01:44,870 --> 00:01:48,320
John: Can you give me some examples of the activities?

27
00:01:48,320 --> 00:01:51,200
Sally: Well, one focus is on science

28
00:01:51,620 --> 00:01:57,190
where we help children to discover and study plants, trees and insects.

29
00:01:57,190 --> 00:02:01,920
Sally: They also collect and analyse data about the things they see.

30
00:02:01,920 --> 00:02:02,730
John: Uhuh.

31
00:02:02,730 --> 00:02:08,665
Sally: Another focus is on geography. The park is a great environment to learn

32
00:02:08,665 --> 00:02:14,600
and practise reading a map and using a compass to navigate around the park.

33
00:02:14,600 --> 00:02:18,510
John: Do you do anything connected with history?

34
00:02:18,510 --> 00:02:20,960
Sally: Yes, we do. For instance

35
00:02:21,340 --> 00:02:26,718
the children can explore how the use of the land has changed over time.

36
00:02:26,718 --> 00:02:29,180
Then there's leisure and tourism.

37
00:02:29,180 --> 00:02:33,310
John: That focuses on your visitors, I would imagine.

38
00:02:33,310 --> 00:02:37,437
Sally: Yes, mostly. The children find out about them,

39
00:02:37,437 --> 00:02:42,940
their requirements, the problems they may cause and how we manage these.

40
00:02:42,940 --> 00:02:47,030
Sally: And another subject we cover is music:

41
00:02:47,030 --> 00:02:52,040
here the children experiment with natural materials to create sounds

42
00:02:52,040 --> 00:02:54,700
and explore rhythm and tempo.

43
00:02:54,700 --> 00:02:56,190
John: That must be fun!

44
00:02:56,190 --> 00:03:02,310
Sally: Most children really enjoy it.

45
00:03:02,310 --> 00:03:07,510
Sally: And of course, all the activities are educational, too.

46
00:03:07,510 --> 00:03:12,140
Learning outside the classroom encourages children to be creative

47
00:03:12,140 --> 00:03:15,830
and to explore and discover for themselves.

48
00:03:15,830 --> 00:03:22,140
John: I would imagine they get a sense of freedom that might not be a normal part of their lives.

49
00:03:22,140 --> 00:03:24,680
Sally: That's right. And very often

50
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:30,919
the children discover that they can do things they didn't know they could do,

51
00:03:30,919 --> 00:03:34,910
and they develop new skills. This gives them greater self-confidence.

52
00:03:34,910 --> 00:03:40,546
John: It sounds great. So, what about the practical side of it?

53
00:03:40,546 --> 00:03:44,000
How much does it cost for a full-day visit

54
00:03:44,600 --> 00:03:49,470
We would expect to bring between 30 and 40 children.

55
00:03:49,470 --> 00:03:56,987
Sally: If there are over 30, it costs £4.95 for each child who attends on the day.

56
00:03:56,987 --> 00:04:01,600
We invoice you afterwards, so you don't pay for children

57
00:04:01,600 --> 00:04:05,340
who can't come because of sickness, for example.

58
00:04:05,340 --> 00:04:09,060
Sally: There's no charge for leaders and other adults

59
00:04:09,380 --> 00:04:11,350
as many as you want to bring.

60
00:04:11,350 --> 00:04:15,197
John: That sounds very fair. Well, thanks for all the information.

61
00:04:15,197 --> 00:04:18,880
I'll need to discuss it with my colleagues

62
00:04:18,880 --> 00:04:22,890
and I hope to get back to you soon to make a booking.

63
00:04:22,890 --> 00:04:26,410
Sally: We'll look forward to hearing from you. Goodbye.

64
00:04:26,410 --> 00:04:30,200
John: Goodbye, and thank you.
